{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgt2XUUy9rv2tVL5Lz9NR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now the picture is quantified. 98.5% of the 464 cut generations end in an exact loop (≥95% of 32-grams repeated from the onset to the end). The loop starts at a median of ~4,100 tokens (10th–90th percentile: 1,300–10,000) and has a short period (median 43 tokens, i.e. one or two lines). 84% of problems loop in both samples, and finished answers (median 4,500 tokens) are essentially always correct. So the model can usually solve the problem; it just falls into a self-copying attractor and never escapes. Next, I want to characterize the loop units: their content (does the repeated line start with \"Wait\"?), their indentation depth (the example was deeply nested), and what happens just before onset. A common trigger, such as deep bullet nesting, would point to a specific mechanism; diverse loop content would point to a general copy circuit.\n\n```python\n# Hypothesis: loops have a stereotyped form (deeply indented bullet lines, often \"Wait\"); characterize loop units.\nimport re\nex = []\nfor r in df[df.cut & df.onset.notna()].itertuples():\n    ids = IDS[(r.id, r.si)]\n    on = int(r.onset); per = int(r.period) if r.period else 40\n    unit = tok.decode(ids[on + 2*per: on + 3*per])\n    pre = tok.decode(ids[max(0, on-60): on])\n    ind = [len(l) - len(l.lstrip(' ')) for l in unit.split('\\n') if l.strip()]\n    ex.append(dict(id=r.id, unit=unit, pre=pre, indent=max(ind) if ind else 0, wait=('Wait' in unit), nl=unit.count('\\n')))\ned = pd.DataFrame(ex)\nprint('share of loop units containing \"Wait\":', ed.wait.mean(), ' indent quantiles:', ed.indent.quantile([.1,.25,.5,.75,.9]).tolist())\nprint('newlines per unit quantiles:', ed.nl.quantile([.1,.5,.9]).tolist())\nfor i in range(0, 60, 6):\n    print(f'[{i}] indent={ed.indent[i]} UNIT:', asc(repr(ed.unit[i][:160])))\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":nu